L.append(lesson(
 'software-engineering','Software engineering','Read quickly, then validate: StampedLock optimistic reads','Use an optimistic read only when you can discard a torn snapshot and retry under a real read lock.','Java concurrency · optimistic read',
 '<p>You glance at a departure board while a worker is changing two fields. The destination may be old while the time is new. A fast glance is useful only if you check that no writer changed the board while you read it.</p><p><code>StampedLock.tryOptimisticRead()</code> gives a stamp without blocking a writer. Read fields into local variables, then call <code>validate(stamp)</code>. If validation fails, take a read lock and read the whole snapshot again.</p>',
 '<p>A routing point starts at <code>x=10</code>. The reader takes an optimistic stamp and sees 10. A writer changes x to 11 before validation. Validation is false, so the reader must discard 10 and reread 11 under a read lock. Publishing the first value before validation is the bug.</p>',
 {'when':'Use optimistic reads for small, read-heavy snapshots where writes are rare and a cheap retry is possible.','example':'A request router frequently reads an immutable-looking pair of capacity fields while a controller updates them occasionally.','decision':'Prefer an ordinary read lock when the invariant is complex, reads have side effects, validation failures are common, or the code cannot keep provisional values private.'},
 'Toggle whether a write lands between the optimistic read and validation. Watch the provisional value either commit or get discarded.',
 'The browser models one integer and one intervening write. Real StampedLock reads can observe inconsistent multi-field state, stamps are not ownership tokens, the lock is not reentrant, and starvation/fairness are not guaranteed.',
 '<div id="world"><label><input id="writeBetween" type="checkbox" checked> writer changes x from 10 to 11 before validate</label><div id="stampWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "function paintStamp(){const changed=writeBetween.checked;stampWorld.innerHTML='<div class=\"row\"><div class=\"node wait\"><strong>provisional read</strong><br>x = 10</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"node '+(changed?'fail':'ok')+'\"><strong>validate</strong><br>'+(changed?'false · writer acquired lock':'true · no writer')+'</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"node ok\"><strong>published</strong><br>x = '+(changed?'11 after locked reread':'10')+'</div></div>';};writeBetween.onchange=paintStamp;paintStamp();",
 [q('A writer acquires the write lock after the optimistic stamp. What does validate return?',['False','True forever','A new value'],0,'A write acquisition since the stamp invalidates the optimistic read.'),q('Why copy fields into locals before validation?',['They are provisional and must not escape until validated','It upgrades the stamp automatically','It makes the lock reentrant'],0,'The optimistic snapshot may be inconsistent and must remain discardable.'),q('When is an ordinary read lock simpler?',['When invariants are complex or validation often fails','Only when there are no writers','Never'],0,'Frequent retries or complex consistency checks erase the optimistic path’s benefit.')],
 [{'code':'var lock = new java.util.concurrent.locks.StampedLock(); int x = 10;','note':'Create the lock and initial value.'},{'code':'long stamp = lock.tryOptimisticRead(); int observed = x;','note':'Read a provisional value without excluding a writer.'},{'code':'long ws = lock.writeLock(); x = 11; lock.unlockWrite(ws);','note':'A writer changes the value before validation.'},{'code':'boolean valid = lock.validate(stamp); int safe = valid ? observed : x;','note':'Validation fails, so the reader discards 10 and uses the reread value.'}],{'valid':'false','safe':'11'},
 [['Java SE 24 StampedLock API','https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/24/docs/api/java.base/java/util/concurrent/locks/StampedLock.html','Java SE 24 API'],['OpenJDK StampedLock source','https://github.com/openjdk/jdk/blob/master/src/java.base/share/classes/java/util/concurrent/locks/StampedLock.java','Current source']],
 'The visual separates provisional from published state. A failed stamp does not “fix” the value; it forces a second, protected read.',
 ['Which read path in your services could keep provisional values private until validation?','What metric would show that failed optimistic reads are making this slower than a normal read lock?'],'java'))

L.append(lesson(
 'distinguished-engineer','Distinguished Engineer','An operational-readiness review is evidence, residual risk, and owners','Turn a launch checklist into a visible risk decision rather than a ceremonial meeting.','Technical leadership · operational readiness',
 '<p>A pilot does not ask, “Did everyone attend the safety meeting?” The pilot checks fuel, weather, instruments, and named actions. A launch review should work the same way.</p><p>An operational-readiness review (ORR) gathers evidence about architecture, operations, event management, and release quality. Unmet items become visible residual risks with owners and dates. The decision is “ship, ship with an explicit condition, or stop”—not “the meeting happened.”</p>',
 '<p>A Kafka platform launch has four claims: restore tested, one-zone capacity proven, pager owner named, and rollback rehearsed. Three pass. The restore test is missing. The board cannot turn that red item green with optimism. It records the risk, owner, due date, and whether the launch can be safely scoped.</p>',
 {'when':'Use an ORR before a new service, major feature, architecture change, or material operating-model change can affect customers.','example':'An EKS-hosted Kafka gateway is moving critical traffic and needs evidence for capacity, recovery, monitoring, deployment, and ownership.','decision':'Keep the checklist specific to recurring failure modes. Require links to evidence, record residual risk, and retire stale questions. For a tiny reversible change, use a smaller readiness check rather than a large board.'},
 'Mark evidence present or missing. The launch outcome follows the highest unresolved risk, not the number of attendees.',
 'This toy uses four equal-weight checks. Real ORRs need risk severity, workload context, exceptions, independent review when warranted, and follow-up tracking. A passed checklist cannot prove every unknown failure has been removed.',
 '<div id="world"><label><input id="restore" type="checkbox"> restore tested</label><label><input id="capacity" type="checkbox" checked> one-zone capacity proven</label><label><input id="owner" type="checkbox" checked> pager owner named</label><label><input id="rollback" type="checkbox" checked> rollback rehearsed</label><div id="orrWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "function paintOrr(){const xs=[['restore',restore.checked],['capacity',capacity.checked],['owner',owner.checked],['rollback',rollback.checked]],missing=xs.filter(x=>!x[1]);orrWorld.innerHTML='<div class=evidence>'+xs.map(x=>'<div class=\"node '+(x[1]?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>'+x[0]+'</strong><br>'+(x[1]?'evidence linked':'risk + owner required')+'</div>').join('')+'</div><p class=\"status '+(missing.length?'fail':'ok')+'\">'+(missing.length?'CONDITIONAL / STOP · unresolved: '+missing.map(x=>x[0]).join(', '):'READY · evidence present; unknown risk still exists')+'</p>';};[restore,capacity,owner,rollback].forEach(x=>x.onchange=paintOrr);paintOrr();",
 [q('The restore checkbox is empty. What should the ORR record?',['Residual risk, owner, due date, and launch decision','Attendance as proof','Green because backups exist'],0,'A backup is not restore evidence; unresolved risk must stay visible.'),q('Why tailor ORR questions to recurring failure modes?',['Evidence then targets known causes of impact','It guarantees no novel incident','It removes ownership'],0,'Specific checks improve prevention without turning the review into an endless generic list.'),q('What is the boundary of a passed ORR?',['It reduces known risk but cannot eliminate unknown failure','It proves the service cannot fail','It replaces monitoring'],0,'Readiness evidence is not certainty.')],
 [{'code':"evidence={'restore':False,'capacity':True,'owner':True,'rollback':True}",'note':'Represent linked evidence, not meeting attendance.'},{'code':"missing=[name for name,ok in evidence.items() if not ok]",'note':'Keep unresolved readiness claims visible.'},{'code':"residual_risk={'item':missing[0],'owner':'storage-team','due':'2026-10-22'}",'note':'Assign the missing test to a named owner and date.'},{'code':"decision='CONDITIONAL' if missing else 'READY'",'note':'The launch decision follows the unresolved risk.'}],{'decision':'CONDITIONAL'},
 [['AWS Operational Readiness Reviews','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/operational-readiness-reviews/wa-operational-readiness-reviews.html','Living AWS Well-Architected guidance'],['AWS ORR tool','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/operational-readiness-reviews/the-orr-tool.html','Living guidance'],['AWS example ORR questions','https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/operational-readiness-reviews/appendix-b-example-orr-questions.html','Living guidance']],
 'The evidence board makes missing proof remain red and attaches a follow-up. It distinguishes “review completed” from “risk accepted with eyes open.”',
 ['Which current launch claim is backed only by confidence rather than linked evidence?','Who may accept each residual-risk tier, and when must an exception expire?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'genai-engineering','GenAI engineering','Show the agent only the tools this run may use','Filter the tool catalog by role and run context before the model chooses an action.','Harness engineering · tool filtering',
 '<p>A technician does not carry every master key into every room. The work order determines which keys come out of the cabinet.</p><p>An agent tool catalog is also an authority surface. OpenAI Agents SDK tools can be conditionally enabled, and MCP servers can apply static or context-aware filters. Hiding <code>delete_customer</code> from a read-only support run reduces both confusion and opportunity. It does not replace authorization inside the tool.</p>',
 '<p>A support agent has five installed tools. For a read-only ticket, the harness exposes <code>lookup_order</code> and <code>search_docs</code>. For an approved refund case, it may also expose <code>create_refund</code>. The backend still verifies customer, amount, policy, and caller at execution time.</p>',
 {'when':'Use tool filtering when agents have different roles, environments, tenants, or phases of work and should not see every installed capability.','example':'A support harness exposes lookup tools during diagnosis and adds a narrowly scoped refund tool only after the workflow reaches an approved refund state.','decision':'Filter before the model call using trusted run context. Keep server-side authorization, argument validation, approval, and audit. If the role logic is unclear, expose the smaller set and fail closed.'},
 'Change the run role. Watch tools disappear from the model-visible rack while the server-side authorization boundary remains.',
 'This local model has three roles and five tools. It does not call the Agents SDK or MCP, prove security, model prompt injection, handle cached tool lists, or test server-side authorization. A hidden tool is smaller exposure, not a complete access-control system.',
 '<div id="world"><label>Run role <select id="agentRole"><option value="reader">read-only support</option><option value="refund">approved refund</option><option value="admin">admin maintenance</option></select></label><div id="toolWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "const allTools=['lookup_order','search_docs','create_refund','delete_customer','rotate_key'],allowed={reader:['lookup_order','search_docs'],refund:['lookup_order','search_docs','create_refund'],admin:['lookup_order','search_docs','rotate_key']};function paintTools(){const yes=allowed[agentRole.value];toolWorld.innerHTML='<div class=toolrack>'+allTools.map(t=>'<div class=\"tool '+(yes.includes(t)?'ok':'off')+'\"><strong>'+t+'</strong><br>'+(yes.includes(t)?'visible to model':'filtered before model call')+'</div>').join('')+'</div><p><strong>Execution rule:</strong> every visible tool still rechecks authorization and arguments.</p>';};agentRole.onchange=paintTools;paintTools();",
 [q('Why hide delete_customer from a read-only run?',['Reduce authority exposure and selection errors','Make backend authorization unnecessary','Improve network bandwidth only'],0,'A smaller catalog narrows what the model can propose.'),q('Where should the filter decision come from?',['Trusted run context and policy','Untrusted tool output','The model asking for more power'],0,'Authority must not be granted by the content being governed.'),q('What must remain inside a visible tool?',['Authorization and argument validation','Nothing; visibility is enough','Only a friendly description'],0,'Catalog filtering cannot enforce tenant, amount, or resource-level policy by itself.')],
 [{'code':"role='reader'",'note':'Trusted run context identifies a read-only support session.'},{'code':"catalog=['lookup_order','search_docs','create_refund','delete_customer']",'note':'The server has more capabilities installed than this run should expose.'},{'code':"visible=[t for t in catalog if t in {'lookup_order','search_docs'}]",'note':'Filter before the model receives the tool list.'},{'code':"execution_guard='required even for '+visible[0]",'note':'The handler still verifies authorization when a visible tool runs.'}],{'visible':['lookup_order','search_docs']},
 [['OpenAI Agents SDK tools guide','https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/guides/tools/','Living documentation checked October 20, 2026'],['OpenAI Agents SDK MCP tool filtering','https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-js/guides/mcp/#tool-filtering','Living documentation checked October 20, 2026']],
 'The rack changes what the model can see before selection. The separate execution rule stays fixed, making defense in depth visible.',
 ['Which agent role in your system currently receives tools it never needs?','What trusted state should determine when a high-impact tool becomes visible?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'technology-breakthroughs','Technology breakthroughs','AEGIS separates deployability from danger in the released model','Understand a 2026 research governance system with a four-way decision and an independent alarm.','New research · published October 13, 2026',
 '<p>A hospital tests a candidate model update. Two different questions matter: “May we deploy the candidate?” and “Is the model already serving patients now at risk?” One answer cannot safely stand in for both.</p><p>The AEGIS research prototype combines data assimilation/retraining, model monitoring, and conditional decisions. It uses four outcomes—APPROVE, CONDITIONAL APPROVAL, CLINICAL REVIEW, and REJECT—plus an independent ALARM for the released model.</p>',
 '<p>Suppose the candidate misses the safety threshold, so it is REJECTED. At the same time, monitoring finds drift in the released model. The combined state is <strong>REJECT + ALARM</strong>: there is no deployable replacement and the current model may be unsafe. That state needs escalation, not a silent fallback.</p>',
 {'when':'Consider this research when designing post-market governance for adaptive, regulated medical AI that changes after initial deployment.','example':'A sepsis model candidate fails validation while the released model’s input distribution is drifting at another hospital.','decision':'Treat AEGIS as a proof-of-concept architecture, not a production medical-control system. Validate clinical endpoints, concept drift, false alarms, human authority, auditability, site transfer, and regulatory obligations independently.'},
 'Set candidate safety and released-model alarm independently. The outcome matrix shows why REJECT and ALARM are separate dimensions.',
 'This browser is an original two-threshold teaching model. It is not the authors’ code, clinical validation, regulatory approval, a drift detector, or a medical device. The paper reports illustrative sepsis and brain-tumor cases; it leaves concept drift for future work.',
 '<div id="world"><label>Candidate score <input id="candidateScore" type="range" min="60" max="100" value="72"></label><label><input id="releasedAlarm" type="checkbox" checked> released model triggers ALARM</label><div id="aegisWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "function paintAegis(){const s=+candidateScore.value,decision=s>=90?'APPROVE':s>=80?'CONDITIONAL APPROVAL':s>=75?'CLINICAL REVIEW':'REJECT',alarm=releasedAlarm.checked;aegisWorld.innerHTML='<div class=matrix><div class=cell><strong>candidate</strong></div><div class=\"cell '+(decision==='REJECT'?'fail':'wait')+'\">score '+s+' → '+decision+'</div><div class=cell><strong>released model</strong></div><div class=\"cell '+(alarm?'fail':'ok')+'\">'+(alarm?'ALARM':'no alarm')+'</div></div><div class=\"alarm '+(decision==='REJECT'&&alarm?'fail':'')+'\"><strong>combined state:</strong> '+decision+' + '+(alarm?'ALARM':'NO ALARM')+'<br>'+(decision==='REJECT'&&alarm?'No safe replacement and current release at risk: escalate.':'Follow the governance path; this toy does not authorize deployment.')+'</div>';};candidateScore.oninput=paintAegis;releasedAlarm.onchange=paintAegis;paintAegis();",
 [q('Why is ALARM independent of the candidate decision?',['The released model can be at risk even when no candidate is deployable','It makes every candidate pass','It measures only training speed'],0,'Candidate readiness and current-release safety are different questions.'),q('What does REJECT + ALARM mean in the example?',['No deployable candidate while the released model is at risk','Automatic approval','The alarm may be ignored'],0,'The combined state requires critical escalation.'),q('What is a stated research boundary?',['The proof-of-concept evaluates covariate shift and leaves concept drift for future work','It is a certified medical device','It removes clinical review'],0,'The paper explicitly limits what its drift scoring establishes.')],
 [{'code':'candidate_score=72\nreleased_alarm=True','note':'Represent the candidate and current release as separate signals.'},{'code':"decision='REJECT' if candidate_score<75 else 'REVIEW'",'note':'The candidate fails the toy deployment threshold.'},{'code':"critical=decision=='REJECT' and released_alarm",'note':'Combine rejection with an independent alarm.'},{'code':"action='ESCALATE' if critical else 'FOLLOW_PATH'",'note':'The critical state cannot silently fall back to the current model.'}],{'action':'ESCALATE'},
 [['Afdideh et al., AEGIS','https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-026-03290-1','Published October 13, 2026'],['AEGIS preprint','https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.22322','Submitted March 20, 2026']],
 'The two-axis matrix prevents a common collapse: candidate quality and released-model danger remain visible at the same time.',
 ['Which monitoring signal in your domain should be independent of candidate promotion?','What clinical or operational evidence is missing before this research architecture could govern a real deployment?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'ci-cd-github-actions','CI/CD & GitHub Actions','A called workflow cannot add permissions its caller withheld','Design reusable workflow chains so token authority only stays the same or shrinks.','Reusable workflows · least privilege',
 '<p>A manager can lend a badge with fewer doors, but the recipient cannot draw a new door on it. Reusable GitHub Actions workflows work the same way.</p><p>In a chain A → B → C, token permissions can be maintained or reduced, not elevated. If A grants <code>contents: read</code> and no <code>packages: write</code>, B or C cannot create that missing permission by declaring it later.</p>',
 '<p>The application workflow A calls build workflow B, which calls publish workflow C. A grants <code>contents: read</code> only. C requests <code>packages: write</code>. Publication fails because the upstream caller never delegated that authority. The fix is not broad write-all; split build and publish, and grant the publish caller exactly the required scope.</p>',
 {'when':'Use this model when central reusable workflows call other workflows across repositories and need cloud, package, release, or repository permissions.','example':'A shared build workflow is read-only, while a separate trusted release workflow publishes an image to GitHub Packages.','decision':'Draw the permission chain from the original caller. Put privileged publishing behind a distinct trusted caller/environment, and grant only the exact scopes needed. Treat secrets and token permissions as separate concerns.'},
 'Change the caller’s package permission. Watch the effective scope shrink through A → B → C even when C asks for write.',
 'This browser models two scopes and three workflows. Real GitHub rules also depend on repository settings, event, fork, actor, secret inheritance, environment protection, OIDC, organization policy, and the exact called-workflow syntax.',
 '<div id="world"><label>Caller A packages permission <select id="callerPerm"><option value="none">none</option><option value="read">read</option><option value="write">write</option></select></label><div id="permissionWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "const rank={none:0,read:1,write:2};function cap(parent,asked){return Object.keys(rank).find(k=>rank[k]===Math.min(rank[parent],rank[asked]));}function paintPerm(){const a=callerPerm.value,b=cap(a,'read'),c=cap(b,'write');permissionWorld.innerHTML='<div class=token-chain><div class=\"token '+(a==='write'?'ok':'wait')+'\"><strong>A caller</strong><br>packages: '+a+'</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=token><strong>B build</strong><br>asks read · gets '+b+'</div><div class=arrow>→</div><div class=\"token '+(c==='write'?'ok':'fail')+'\"><strong>C publish</strong><br>asks write · gets '+c+'</div></div><p>'+(c==='write'?'Publish has the delegated write scope.':'Publish cannot elevate the chain; split/use a trusted caller with exact write scope.')+'</p>';};callerPerm.onchange=paintPerm;paintPerm();",
 [q('A grants packages: read. C asks for packages: write. What is C’s effective maximum?',['read','write','admin'],0,'Nested reusable workflows cannot elevate permissions above the caller chain.'),q('Why split build and publish callers?',['The common build stays read-only while a trusted release path gets narrow write authority','To hide logs','To make every workflow write-all'],0,'Separation keeps routine code away from publishing authority.'),q('Do inherited secrets automatically grant token permissions?',['No; secrets and GITHUB_TOKEN permissions are separate','Yes, always','Only on weekends'],0,'Passing a secret does not manufacture a missing GITHUB_TOKEN scope.')],
 [{'code':"rank={'none':0,'read':1,'write':2}", 'note':'Order the toy permission levels.'},{'code':"caller='read'\nbuild_request='read'",'note':'The caller delegates only read authority.'},{'code':"build=min(rank[caller],rank[build_request])",'note':'The build receives at most what the caller granted.'},{'code':"publish=min(build,rank['write'])",'note':'The publisher asks for write but remains capped at read.'}],{'publish':1},
 [['GitHub: Reuse workflows','https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/reuse-automations/reuse-workflows','Living documentation checked October 20, 2026'],['GitHub: Reusing workflow configurations','https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/reusing-workflow-configurations','Living documentation checked October 20, 2026'],['GitHub workflow syntax permissions','https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/workflow-syntax#permissions','Living documentation checked October 20, 2026']],
 'The badge chain keeps the caller’s ceiling visible. Each hop can retain or remove doors; none can invent a new one.',
 ['Which reusable workflow chain currently relies on an implicit permission you have not traced from the caller?','Where should a privileged publish workflow be separated from untrusted build execution?']))

L.append(lesson(
 'apis-microservices','APIs & Microservices','One URL, two languages: Vary is part of the cache key','Prevent a shared cache from serving the English representation to a French request.','HTTP caching · representation selection',
 '<p>A restaurant uses one menu number for English and French. If the waiter files only by menu number, the next guest may receive the wrong language. The language choice must be part of the filing key.</p><p>HTTP <code>Vary</code> tells a cache which request headers selected the stored representation. With <code>Vary: Accept-Language</code>, <code>/help</code> in English and French occupy separate variants. Without that dimension, a shared cache can replay the wrong body.</p>',
 '<p>Request 1 asks for <code>/help</code> with <code>Accept-Language: en</code>; the origin returns “Reset password” and <code>Vary: Accept-Language</code>. Request 2 asks for French. A compliant cache does not reuse the English variant because the nominated request header differs; it fetches/stores “Réinitialiser le mot de passe.”</p>',
 {'when':'Use Vary when the same URI has cacheable representations selected by request headers such as Accept-Encoding or Accept-Language.','example':'A public help API returns localized text from one URI through a shared CDN.','decision':'Vary only on headers that genuinely select representation. High-cardinality dimensions fragment the cache. Personalized or sensitive responses may need private/no-store behavior instead of a huge Vary key.'},
 'Toggle whether the origin sends Vary. The cache either keeps two language slots or incorrectly reuses the first body.',
 'This browser models one shared cache, one URI, two languages, and no freshness/revalidation. Real caches also consider method, target URI, cache controls, authorization, validators, status codes, CDN configuration, and header normalization.',
 '<div id="world"><label><input id="varyOn" type="checkbox" checked> response includes Vary: Accept-Language</label><button id="resetCache">Replay en then fr</button><div id="cacheWorld" class="world"></div></div>',
 "function paintCache(){const vary=varyOn.checked,keyEn=vary?'/help|en':'/help',keyFr=vary?'/help|fr':'/help',fr=keyFr===keyEn?'Reset password':'Réinitialiser le mot de passe';cacheWorld.innerHTML='<div class=cache><div class=cell><strong>request</strong></div><div class=cell>en</div><div class=cell>fr</div><div class=cell><strong>cache key</strong></div><div class=cell>'+keyEn+'</div><div class=cell>'+keyFr+'</div><div class=cell><strong>body served</strong></div><div class=\"cell ok\">Reset password</div><div class=\"cell '+(vary?'ok':'fail')+'\">'+fr+'</div></div><p>'+(vary?'Header values differ, so the French request cannot reuse the English variant.':'Same key: the second request receives the cached English body.')+'</p>';};varyOn.onchange=paintCache;resetCache.onclick=paintCache;paintCache();",
 [q('What does Vary: Accept-Language change?',['The cache’s representation-matching key','The URI path itself','The user’s authorization'],0,'The nominated request header must match before a stored response is reused without revalidation.'),q('What happens without Vary in the example?',['The French request can receive the cached English body','The cache becomes encrypted','The origin changes method'],0,'Both requests collapse to the same cache key.'),q('Why not Vary on every request header?',['High-cardinality keys fragment the cache and reduce hits','It makes HTTP invalid','It guarantees freshness'],0,'Only representation-selecting dimensions belong in the secondary key.')],
 [{'code':"uri='/help'\nfirst_language='en'\nfirst_body='Reset password'",'note':'The English response fills the first cache entry.'},{'code':"vary=['Accept-Language']",'note':'The response names the request header that selected the representation.'},{'code':"cache_key=(uri,first_language if 'Accept-Language' in vary else None)", 'note':'The cache key now includes the language dimension.'},{'code':"second_key=(uri,'fr' if 'Accept-Language' in vary else None)\ncache_hit=second_key==cache_key",'note':'The French request does not match the English variant.'}],{'cache_hit':False},
 [['RFC 9111: HTTP Caching','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html#section-4.1','Published June 2022'],['RFC 9110: Vary','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html#field.vary','Published June 2022'],['RFC 9211: Cache-Status','https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9211.html','Published June 2022']],
 'The table makes the hidden secondary key visible. The wrong-language failure appears exactly when two representations collapse into one slot.',
 ['Which response header selects a representation in one of your APIs today, and is it reflected in the cache key?','How would Cache-Status and an origin request ID help distinguish an origin bug from a cache-key bug?']))

